shift s = 1.0: t = 0.1->0.10, 0.3->0.30, 0.5->0.50, 0.7->0.70, 0.9->0.90
shift s = 3.0: t = 0.1->0.25, 0.3->0.56, 0.5->0.75, 0.7->0.87, 0.9->0.96
with s = 3 half of the steps (t <= 0.5) are spent where the shifted time is below 0.75 that is, there is more emphasis on high-noise levels where global structure forms
